INT. NIGHT TRAIN DINING CAR - NIGHT

A narrow dining car, white tablecloths, brass lamps. The rails clack beneath. Outside the window, the dark country streams by.

CAPTAIN LEOPOLD "POLLY" SCHRADER, 50s, stout, a retired customs officer in a three-piece suit, cuts into a steak with ceremony. Across from him, MISS VERONIKA HAAS, 30s, silver cigarette holder, sips from a thin glass and watches him over the rim. At the next table, alone, sits their waiter-turned-witness, KAZIMIR "KAZ" BLUME, 20s, in a white jacket, polishing the same spoon for the fourth time.

POLLY
You don't eat, Miss Haas.

VERONIKA
I never do at night.

POLLY
A woman who doesn't eat at night is a woman with something on her mind.

VERONIKA
Or on her conscience.

The train lurches. Her glass slides an inch. Polly catches it before it falls, and holds it a moment too long in his hand.

POLLY
Careful.

VERONIKA
Thank you, Captain.

KAZ
(from the next table)
Sir, if I may. The steak. Is it satisfactory?

POLLY
Kaz, my boy, the steak is excellent.

KAZ
I'm glad, sir. I prepared it myself.

Polly's knife stops.

POLLY
Did you.

KAZ
It was the last of the lot. The cook was indisposed.

Veronika sets her glass down very softly. The lamps shake. The knife rests on the plate. Polly dabs his lips with a napkin, rises to his full, unsteady height, and clutches the table.

POLLY (CONT'D)
Veronika. I feel--

She is already on her feet. She catches his arm as he buckles, easing him into the chair. Kaz has put down the spoon.

VERONIKA
Kaz. The window.

KAZ
Yes, miss.

He slides the sash up. Night air floods the car. Polly breathes, hard, his eyes on her.

POLLY
(whispering)
Veronika. The steak.

VERONIKA
I know, Polly.

POLLY
You?

VERONIKA
Not me, darling.

She turns, slowly, to Kaz, who stands in the open window with the spoon in his hand and a different expression than before.

KAZ
(quietly)
Miss Haas. You weren't meant to be at this table.
